    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists are traditionally recommended as first-line monotherapy for type 2 diabetes; metformin remains the universal first-line pharmacological treatment alongside lifestyle changes .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists are traditionally recommended as first-line monotherapy for type 2 diabetes"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "However, both classes are frequently prioritized as second-line agents\u2014or even initiated alongside metformin\u2014based on specific patient comorbidities, cardiovascular risk, and organ protection needs.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Core Differences in Selection"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Instead of choosing one universally, medical guidelines (such as the American Diabetes Association) guide selection based on what dominant health condition the patient has:",
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Choose an SGLT2 Inhibitor (e.g., empagliflozin, canagliflozin, dapagliflozin) if the patient has heart failure (especially with reduced ejection fraction) or chronic kidney disease (CKD). SGLT2i provide superior reductions in heart failure hospitalizations and slow the progression of kidney disease.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Choose a GLP-1 Receptor Agonist (e.g., semaglutide, liraglutide) if the patient has predominant atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD), high risk of stroke/MACE (major adverse cardiovascular events), or needs more potent HbA1c lowering and weight loss. GLP-1s are generally more powerful for blood sugar reduction and substantial weight reduction.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4,
              5,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Comparison at a Glance"
      },
